    description: "## Problem\n\n`hypergumbo routes` returns \"No API routes found in the behavior map.\" even when the behavior map contains many endpoint-shaped symbols of other kinds. On hypergumbo's own self-analysis (3.0.0):\n\n```\n$ hypergumbo routes\nNo API routes found in the behavior map.\n```\n\n…while the same behavior map contains 28 `websocket_endpoint`, 19 `graphql_resolver`, 35 `db_query`, 27 `event_subscriber`, 39 `event_publisher`, 63 `mq_subscriber`, 54 `mq_publisher` nodes. The user has no signal that any of these were detected.\n\n## Why this is the right narrowness for `routes` but the wrong UX\n\nPer `cli.py:1612-1625`, `cmd_routes` deliberately filters to only:\n- `node.meta.concepts[].concept == \"route\"` (YAML framework pattern enrichment)\n- `node.kind == \"route\"` (analyzers that emit `route` symbols directly, e.g., Go)\n\nThat narrow definition is correct — websocket endpoints, graphql resolvers, and message-queue publishers/subscribers are semantically distinct from HTTP routes and shouldn't be conflated by default. But the empty-result UX is a blind alley: the user walks away thinking the analysis found no endpoints, when in fact several non-route endpoint kinds exist in the same behavior map.\n\n## Fix shape\n\nWhen `cmd_routes` would print \"No API routes found\", scan the loaded behavior map for related endpoint-shaped node kinds and append a hint:\n\n```\nNo HTTP routes found in the behavior map.\n\nThe behavior map does contain other endpoint-shaped symbols:\n  - 28 websocket_endpoint nodes\n  - 19 graphql_resolver nodes\n  - 35 db_query nodes\n  - 63 mq_subscriber / 54 mq_publisher nodes (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS, Redis)\n\nTo inspect them, view the JSON output (`hypergumbo run`) or use\n`hypergumbo explain <name>` for individual symbols.\n```\n\nThe set of \"related\" kinds to scan: `{websocket_endpoint, graphql_resolver, db_query, event_publisher, event_subscriber, mq_publisher, mq_subscriber, http_client, subprocess_call}`. Same set already used elsewhere in the codebase as \"non-route endpoint-shaped symbols\" — pull from a shared constant if one exists, or define one in `cli.py` near `cmd_routes`.\n\nOnly emit the hint when at least one such node is present; otherwise the existing single-line message stands.\n\n## Implementation footprint\n\n~15 LOC in `cmd_routes` (cli.py:1671-1678 area):\n```python\nif not routes:\n    print(\"No HTTP routes found in the behavior map.\")\n    related_counts = _count_related_endpoints(nodes)  # NEW\n    if related_counts:\n        print()\n        print(\"The behavior map does contain other endpoint-shaped symbols:\")\n        for kind, count in related_counts:\n            print(f\"  - {count} {kind} nodes\")\n        print()\n        print(\"To inspect them, view the JSON output or use `hypergumbo explain <name>`.\")\n    ...\n```\n\nPlus a small helper `_count_related_endpoints(nodes) -> list[tuple[str, int]]` (~10 LOC).\n\n## Test plan\n\n- Behavior map with 0 routes and ≥1 websocket_endpoint → hint includes that line.\n- Behavior map with 0 routes and 0 of any related kind → existing single-line message preserved exactly (back-compat with any consumer that greps for it).\n- Behavior map with ≥1 route → hint not emitted (the existing happy path is unchanged).\n\n100% coverage.\n\n## Interaction with parent gap (1)\n\nThe Starlette extractor work (sibling `todo_hard` item) will move 7 HTTP routes from invisible to visible on hypergumbo's self-analysis. After that lands, `hypergumbo routes` on hypergumbo will show 7 routes from serve.py — so the empty-result hint won't fire on hypergumbo. The hint still matters for repos that have e.g. only websocket endpoints, only message queues, or use frameworks not yet supported by the YAML/extractor system.\n\nThese two items are independent (no `isbefore`); either can land first.\n\n## Source\n\nSelf-analysis dogfooding session, hypergumbo 3.0.0 @ d5e3941c6, 2026-04-29."  # 817f
